1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
An age-old battle.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Good versus evil. Criminal versus the law.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Now, a new player is balancing the scales of justice.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Science. Hair follicles. Microfibers. DNA.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:22,000
They're unmasking murderers. Solving old crimes. Setting innocence free.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, Forensic Science on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,940
The modern era of crime fighting began a century ago in the consulting room of a London physician.

8
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:48,940
With his medical practice failing, Dr. Arthur Conan Doyle turned to writing stories.

9
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:58,460
In 1887, he invented the most famous fictional character of all time, and with him, a new scientific field.

10
00:00:58,461 --> 00:01:03,460
Consulting detective Sherlock Holmes deciphered physical clues,

11
00:01:03,461 --> 00:01:08,460
a revolutionary approach to solving crimes.

12
00:01:08,461 --> 00:01:13,460
What do you think? There's been murder done. The murder was a man.

13
00:01:13,461 --> 00:01:17,460
He was in the prime of life, six feet in height,

14
00:01:17,461 --> 00:01:22,460
has small feet, wears square-toed boots, limps slightly on the left foot

15
00:01:22,461 --> 00:01:25,460
and smokes Trichinopoli cigars.

16
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:28,440
The early police work was totally dependent on informers.

17
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:31,320
And then Arthur Conan Doyle came along and he said,

18
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:36,240
you don't need informers, you don't need to hit people over the head with rubber pipes to make them confess.

19
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:37,920
They're going to leave evidence behind.

20
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:44,440
Once Sherlock Holmes showed what was possible,

21
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:48,360
police officers at Scotland Yard followed his lead.

22
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:51,740
Forensic science was born.

23
00:01:55,460 --> 00:02:07,460
Nineteenth century policemen struggled with the difficulty of reliably identifying individuals.

24
00:02:07,461 --> 00:02:12,460
Photography, body measurements, even branding offenders.

25
00:02:12,461 --> 00:02:15,460
All these methods fell short.

26
00:02:15,461 --> 00:02:24,460
Then in the 1890s, British scientists developed the long sought, foolproof means of identification, fingerprints.

27
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:29,420
They organized the seemingly random patterns into three categories,

28
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:33,360
loops, arches, and swirls,

29
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:36,460
allowing prints to be classified and compared.

30
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:44,600
By 1903, both Scotland Yard and the New York City Police Department

31
00:02:44,601 --> 00:02:49,100
fingerprinted suspects, a practice quickly adopted the world over.

32
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:53,080
In the decades to come, even public enemy number one

33
00:02:53,081 --> 00:02:56,020
would learn the power of the new technology.

34
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:02,160
Don Dillinger, hoodlum, bandit, jailbreaker, bank robber and murderer

35
00:03:02,161 --> 00:03:04,700
was headed for Indiana to be tried for murder.

36
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:07,080
A shudder of relief thrilled the country

37
00:03:07,081 --> 00:03:10,280
for the entire nation had hoped for the capture of this gunman

38
00:03:10,281 --> 00:03:14,120
who from petty obscurity had leaped to shameful notoriety

39
00:03:14,121 --> 00:03:17,280
as public enemy number one within a few brief weeks.

40
00:03:17,281 --> 00:03:25,480
Dillinger's wild rampage of robberies and murders had terrorized the Midwest.

41
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:30,100
Awaiting trial, he was held in a supposedly escape-proof jail

42
00:03:30,101 --> 00:03:33,920
and guarded by an army of gun-toting FBI men.

43
00:03:35,460 --> 00:03:40,020
He boldly bluffed his way out with a wooden gun and was back on the loose.

44
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:44,900
Desperate to conceal his identity, he dyed his hair, grew a mustache,

45
00:03:44,901 --> 00:03:48,740
and had a surgeon alter his face and his fingertips.

46
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:55,000
Throughout history, people have tried to obscure or destroy their fingerprints.

47
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:57,220
There's the great legendary story of John Dillinger,

48
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:01,920
who paid a doctor $5,000 to treat his fingerprints with acid.

49
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:05,140
He did successfully obscure the center of the fingerprints,

50
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:08,940
but there were ridges around those points that were easily identifiable as his.

51
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:15,280
For months, Dillinger successfully eluded a nationwide manhunt.

52
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:18,840
Then, betrayed by one of his many girlfriends,

53
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:24,420
he was gunned down by G-Men as he emerged from the Biograph Theater in Chicago.

54
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:29,960
The bullet-riddled corpse bore little resemblance to a figure known by all.

55
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:35,620
But the outer ridges of his fingers proved that it was John Dillinger.

56
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:50,440
Forty years later, the next generation of fingerprint technology would expose one of

57
00:04:50,441 --> 00:05:01,320
the century's most notorious assassins.

58
00:05:01,321 --> 00:05:05,489
On April 4th, 1968, civil rights leader Martin Luther King Jr.

59
00:05:05,490 --> 00:05:07,640
was shot to death on the balcony

60
00:05:07,641 --> 00:05:10,960
of a Memphis motel.

61
00:05:10,961 --> 00:05:15,760
In a room across the street, police recovered the killer's rifle.

62
00:05:15,761 --> 00:05:19,840
Nearly wiped clean, the rifle butt contained the partial print

63
00:05:19,841 --> 00:05:22,860
of a single finger.

64
00:05:22,861 --> 00:05:29,400
With few other leads, a massive effort was made to identify the print.

65
00:05:29,401 --> 00:05:33,060
The FBI examiners literally broke up

66
00:05:33,061 --> 00:05:37,260
into groups of thousands of individuals

67
00:05:37,261 --> 00:05:40,180
And one by one, they went through those fingerprint cards,

68
00:05:40,460 --> 00:05:45,300
manually searching the fingerprints found at that scene

69
00:05:45,301 --> 00:05:49,040
against millions of card files.

70
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:54,440
But the FBI files contained over 50 million prints,

71
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:57,240
too many for any manual search.

72
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,800
For the first time in fingerprinting history,

73
00:06:00,801 --> 00:06:03,760
a rudimentary computer narrowed the field.

74
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:08,360
It selected out the fingerprint cards of wanted felons,

75
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:10,820
a manageable 2,000 names.

76
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:14,400
They were going to go through every one of those 2,000 cards

77
00:06:14,401 --> 00:06:15,720
and see if they could make a match.

78
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:19,160
Just after they started, somebody said, I got him.

79
00:06:19,900 --> 00:06:21,880
And everybody laughed and assumed he was kidding.

80
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:24,200
He said, no, I'm not kidding. This is him. This is the guy.

81
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:26,780
It was maybe the third or fourth card they looked at,

82
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:29,920
and it was James Earl Ray, and that's how he was identified.

83
00:06:29,921 --> 00:06:36,920
Ray's prints were traced to maps, hotel rooms, and car keys across the south.

84
00:06:36,921 --> 00:06:43,920
Investigators followed a trail of fake IDs from city to city and across the Atlantic.

85
00:06:43,921 --> 00:06:50,920
and across the Atlantic.

86
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:03,920
Finally, the FBI and Interpol tracked him to London where he was apprehended.

87
00:07:03,921 --> 00:07:10,920
Ray pleaded guilty to killing King and was sentenced to 99 years in prison.

88
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:22,460
In recent years, computers have evolved into an essential part of fingerprinting technology.

89
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:32,860
The Automated Fingerprint Identification System, or AFIS, compares the ridges and swirls of millions of prints at incredible speed.

90
00:07:32,861 --> 00:07:46,839
When a latent fingerprint is found at the scene of a crime, it can be lifted, imaged, searched against a database of millions of individuals, and with a matter of

91
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:49,600
minutes, identify an individual.

92
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:58,060
New imaging techniques allow modern investigators to detect previously invisible prints.

93
00:07:58,061 --> 00:08:01,380
It used to be at a crime scene

94
00:08:01,381 --> 00:08:03,840
they'd turn on all the lights as brightly as they could

95
00:08:03,841 --> 00:08:05,700
they would shed some light on the crime

96
00:08:05,701 --> 00:08:08,400
now what they do is they turn out the lights

97
00:08:08,401 --> 00:08:10,220
and they put on infrared detectors

98
00:08:10,221 --> 00:08:11,900
heat detectors

99
00:08:11,901 --> 00:08:13,960
they use all kinds of different devices

100
00:08:13,961 --> 00:08:16,200
to find things that can't be seen by the naked eye

101
00:08:16,201 --> 00:08:18,820
but which may very well be good lead evidence

102
00:08:18,821 --> 00:08:22,920
New technologies can solve old mysteries

103
00:08:22,921 --> 00:08:25,660
In 1982

104
00:08:25,661 --> 00:08:27,180
Valerian Trefa

105
00:08:27,181 --> 00:08:29,780
Romanian Orthodox Bishop of Cleveland,

106
00:08:30,180 --> 00:08:33,760
was accused of collaborating with the Nazis in World War II.

107
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:38,560
The case hinged on a key piece of evidence,

108
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:41,660
a 1942 postcard from Trifa,

109
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:44,940
pledging loyalty to SS Chief Henrik Himmler.

110
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:48,440
Trifa denied any contact with Himmler,

111
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:50,700
calling the postcard a forgery.

112
00:08:52,060 --> 00:08:55,060
The West German government permitted the FBI

113
00:08:55,061 --> 00:08:58,640
to inspect the document on the condition that it not be damaged.

114
00:09:00,220 --> 00:09:02,900
Forced to forego their normal methods,

115
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:06,800
agents examined the card with a machine new to the FBI lab.

116
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:09,300
A laser.

117
00:09:11,620 --> 00:09:13,860
Under an intense laser beam,

118
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:18,840
human oil soaked into the paper 40 years earlier became visible.

119
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:23,820
The faint outlines of a hidden fingerprint came to life.

120
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:30,000
It matched the prints on Trifa's U.S. citizenship papers, and he was deported.

121
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:39,220
A 1940s criminal undone by 1980s technology.

122
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:48,980
Like Trifa, many criminals are tripped up by clues they had no idea they were leaving.

123
00:09:48,981 --> 00:09:52,580
as the infamous Atlanta child killer would learn

124
00:09:52,581 --> 00:09:56,780
in the right hand the tiniest thread of evidence

125
00:09:56,781 --> 00:09:58,980
can trap a serial killer.

126
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:03,620
You're watching Forensic Science,

127
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:06,360
the crime fighter's weapon on Modern Models.

128
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:13,560
An old Chinese proverb states that everything

129
00:10:13,561 --> 00:10:16,480
may depend on the difference between two hairs.

130
00:10:16,481 --> 00:10:25,180
For modern criminologists, the telling similarities between hairs and between fibers speak volumes.

131
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:35,060
The last 50 years has seen an explosion in the manufacture of man-made fibers,

132
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:39,760
creating a boom for fashion designers and crime fighters.

133
00:10:39,761 --> 00:10:48,300
beginning in July 1979

134
00:10:48,301 --> 00:10:51,860
a series of murders terrorized the citizens of Atlanta

135
00:10:51,861 --> 00:10:55,600
for almost two agonizing years

136
00:10:55,601 --> 00:10:58,100
the strangled bodies of young men and boys

137
00:10:58,101 --> 00:11:00,700
appeared at the rate of more than one a month

138
00:11:00,701 --> 00:11:05,000
all the victims were African Americans

139
00:11:05,001 --> 00:11:08,800
raising the ugly specter of a racist serial killer

140
00:11:09,560 --> 00:11:11,160
Fear and panic gripped the city.

141
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:17,880
For 16 months, the puzzling children's case has unraveled with almost horrifying certainty,

142
00:11:18,080 --> 00:11:21,060
producing scenes of small bodies draped in white sheets.

143
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:26,860
Since July of 1979, 11 bodies have been discovered and four children have been reported missing.

144
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:29,280
In 1979, there were four deaths.

145
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:37,640
The only physical evidence connecting the killings were tiny violet and greenish-yellow carpet fibers

146
00:11:37,641 --> 00:11:39,480
found on several victims.

147
00:11:40,940 --> 00:11:45,300
A special FBI Atlanta task force began a massive effort

148
00:11:45,301 --> 00:11:48,160
to trace the fibers to their origins.

149
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:55,620
The carpet fibers were quite unusual.

150
00:11:56,900 --> 00:11:59,620
They had unusual characteristics, unusual properties.

151
00:11:59,880 --> 00:12:04,000
We had a difficult time identifying who manufactured these fibers.

152
00:12:04,001 --> 00:12:13,940
In a way, that's good, because the more uncommon a particular item is, the smaller the chance that you're going to find it just in a coincidental residence or a car.

153
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:21,466
The only tentative evidence that may connect him, and I emphasize may connect him to the cases, is some fiber evidence.

154
00:12:21,467 --> 00:12:22,180
Is that right?

155
00:12:22,740 --> 00:12:27,920
When the press made the fiber evidence public, the killer responded by changing his methods.

156
00:12:27,921 --> 00:12:35,160
It began stripping victims and dumping their bodies in rivers, presumably to remove any telltale fibers.

157
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:42,120
Police countered by staking out the major rivers in the Atlanta metropolitan area.

158
00:12:43,260 --> 00:12:50,320
On May 22, 1981, a surveillance team hidden beneath a bridge spanning the Chattahoochee River

159
00:12:50,321 --> 00:12:55,420
heard a loud splash followed by a car slowly driving off the bridge.

160
00:12:55,421 --> 00:13:00,500
The African-American driver was stopped and arrested.

161
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:03,060
His name, Wayne Williams.

162
00:13:06,980 --> 00:13:10,360
Two days later, the naked body of the latest victim

163
00:13:10,361 --> 00:13:13,120
was pulled from the river one mile downstream.

164
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:17,200
In the corpse's hair, one greenish-yellow fiber.

165
00:13:19,160 --> 00:13:23,300
Agents searched Williams' home and discovered a bedroom carpet of the same color.

166
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:28,340
It strands an exact match of the fibers found on so many victims.

167
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:32,320
It was the first of many matches that proved his guilt.

168
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:39,300
They associated Wayne Williams through dog hairs, through carpet hairs,

169
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:42,640
through all kinds of fibers from clothing.

170
00:13:42,641 --> 00:13:47,800
It was the first time in history that a major prosecution

171
00:13:47,801 --> 00:13:50,800
had been based almost solely on hair and fiber evidence.

172
00:13:50,801 --> 00:13:56,560
Williams was convicted of two of the murders and sentenced to life in jail.

173
00:13:57,700 --> 00:14:02,240
This landmark prosecution boosted the importance of hair and fiber evidence,

174
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:06,680
leading to its use even in cases where the victims were never recovered.

175
00:14:06,681 --> 00:14:20,681
One of the extraordinary uses of hair and fiber evidence was in solving the kidnapping of a

176
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:27,041
little girl named Melissa Brennan.

177
00:14:27,042 --> 00:14:29,860
She was with her mother at a Christmas party, her mother turned

178
00:14:29,861 --> 00:14:34,943
around and she was gone and she was never seen again.

179
00:14:34,944 --> 00:14:38,300
December 3rd 1989 began as a joyous

180
00:14:38,301 --> 00:14:42,940
Christmas celebration for a five-year-old Melissa Brannon of Fairfax, Virginia.

181
00:14:42,941 --> 00:14:53,232
At 10 p.m., Melissa suddenly vanished from the party.

182
00:14:53,233 --> 00:14:59,640
Virginia police immediately began

183
00:14:59,641 --> 00:15:05,260
an exhaustive effort to find her, stopping nearby motorists, scouring the area for clues.

184
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:12,400
Throughout that frigid wintry night and into the next day, police, soldiers, and volunteers

185
00:15:12,401 --> 00:15:16,860
searched the woods and rivers for any trace of the little girl.

186
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:26,700
In the hours immediately after Melissa's disappearance, police questioned those present

187
00:15:26,701 --> 00:15:27,592
at the party.

188
00:15:27,593 --> 00:15:33,560
One man, Cal Hughes, had left at 10 p.m., although no one saw him with the little girl.

189
00:15:37,160 --> 00:15:43,560
When police reached Hughes at his home at 1 a.m., he was busy washing his clothes from that evening,

190
00:15:44,180 --> 00:15:46,080
including his belt and shoes.

191
00:15:46,081 --> 00:15:54,120
When Hughes denied that Melissa had ever been in his car

192
00:15:54,121 --> 00:15:59,360
FBI agents used tape to collect hundreds of fibers from the vehicle

193
00:15:59,361 --> 00:16:07,640
FBI Special Agent Doug Diedrich examined the fibers

194
00:16:07,641 --> 00:16:11,080
With a young daughter of his own the same age as Melissa

195
00:16:11,081 --> 00:16:14,320
Diedrich toiled with an extra measure of determination

196
00:16:14,321 --> 00:16:18,940
He quickly identified black rabbit hairs from the car seat,

197
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:21,960
matching them to a coat worn by Melissa's mother.

198
00:16:23,860 --> 00:16:28,740
The tiny strands had traveled from mother to daughter to Cal Hugh's car.

199
00:16:34,100 --> 00:16:37,160
The match put Melissa in Hugh's car,

200
00:16:37,460 --> 00:16:40,120
but the evidence was too slim to arrest him.

201
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:42,400
Diedrich faced a dilemma,

202
00:16:42,401 --> 00:16:45,080
with Melissa and her clothes still missing,

203
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:47,040
how could he make further matches?

204
00:16:48,460 --> 00:16:51,180
Then he hit upon the unprecedented idea

205
00:16:51,181 --> 00:16:53,800
of recreating the Sesame Street outfit

206
00:16:53,801 --> 00:16:55,540
Melissa had worn that night.

207
00:16:58,340 --> 00:17:02,020
The clothes were traced to a JCPenney mail-order catalog

208
00:17:02,021 --> 00:17:04,880
and arrangements made to obtain a duplicate set.

209
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:09,740
Late one afternoon, Federal Express arrived

210
00:17:09,741 --> 00:17:11,360
with a package for Agent Diedrich.

211
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:17,800
He removed a thread from the blue sweater and compared it to a blue fiber from the car.

212
00:17:26,280 --> 00:17:31,100
And there was this feeling of exhilaration that he had made contact with the victim.

213
00:17:31,220 --> 00:17:37,380
In some way, the victim was talking to him, was giving him the evidence that he needed to put her in the front seat of the car.

214
00:17:37,381 --> 00:17:46,320
Diedrich would make over 7,000 comparisons between similar blue fabrics without detecting another match.

215
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,860
Proving the significance of his initial finding.

216
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:54,840
He uncovered other matches as well, including red threads from the skirt.

217
00:17:56,100 --> 00:18:00,120
And in a critical deduction, he showed that the number of fibers in the car

218
00:18:00,121 --> 00:18:03,240
were strong evidence that Melissa removed her jacket.

219
00:18:03,241 --> 00:18:11,240
Although she has yet to be found, Cal Hughes was convicted of abduction and sentenced to 50 years in prison.

220
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:23,240
Melissa's tragedy stunned her family and her community.

221
00:18:23,241 --> 00:18:30,240
Just one year earlier, the global community was shocked by a crime that shattered hundreds of families.

222
00:18:30,241 --> 00:18:30,981
families.

223
00:18:30,982 --> 00:18:37,240
It began in a small Scottish town and it ended as the greatest investigation

224
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:42,640
in crime history.

225
00:18:42,641 --> 00:18:47,640
You're watching Forensic Science, the crime fighter's weapon on Modern Marlowe's.

226
00:18:47,641 --> 00:18:54,640
On November 1, 1955, a United Airlines flight en route from Denver to Portland exploded

227
00:18:54,641 --> 00:18:59,320
to Portland exploded in midair, killing all 44 people aboard.

228
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:06,440
For the first time in U.S. history, a civilian aircraft had fallen prey to a bomber.

229
00:19:17,840 --> 00:19:22,840
A few days later, police arrested 23-year-old Jack Gilbert Graham,

230
00:19:22,841 --> 00:19:27,680
who admitted to planting a bomb in the suitcase of one of the passengers.

231
00:19:28,480 --> 00:19:30,740
That passenger was his mother.

232
00:19:31,680 --> 00:19:34,520
His motive? Collecting on her travel insurance.

233
00:19:35,780 --> 00:19:37,940
Graham was executed for his crimes,

234
00:19:38,300 --> 00:19:41,800
but he had inaugurated a new era of mass murder.

235
00:19:42,220 --> 00:19:47,060
In the 1960s and 70s, a series of bombings rocked the U.S.

236
00:19:47,960 --> 00:19:53,480
The FBI responded by creating a special explosives unit in 1972.

237
00:19:54,640 --> 00:20:01,560
In recent years, that unit has battled bombers far more sophisticated than Jack Gilbert Graham.

238
00:20:17,060 --> 00:20:21,060
The technology of terrorism is escalating.

239
00:20:21,061 --> 00:20:26,060
I think the technology of investigating may be escalating faster.

